{"type":"message_update","usage":{"input":2410,"output":5845,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":4588,"totalTokens":8255,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"assistantMessageEvent":{"type":"thinking_end","contentIndex":0,"content":"Let me check each item.\n\nQ001: \"Killed Shasshhiy'Kaish after letting her grow as powerful as possible.\" → \"在莎西·凯希成长到尽可能强大之后杀死她。\" - correct.\n\nQ002: Long text. Check details. \"That snake. That addlepated beardling.\" → \"那个毒蛇。那个没脑子的毛脸小子。\" ok. \"coin-hounding, blackhearted, stump-kneed dwarf\" → \"追着钱跑、心肠漆黑、短腿跛脚的矮人\" ok. \"able to read the sign of the tavern in Last Hope from the tavern in Derth\" → \"坐在德斯镇的酒馆里就能看清最后的希望城酒馆的招牌\" ok. \"I'll be lucky if I can ever use my left arm again\" → \"我这辈子还能用左臂就算走运了\" ok. \"I'm effectively dead wood to the team\" → ok. \"Sodelost\" → \"苏达罗斯特\" ok. \"lead us up this forsaken mountain to die just so he could rifle through our pockets\" → ok. \"I keep telling Relle\" → ok.\n\nQ003: ok. \"Please!\" → \"求求你！\" ok.\n\nQ004: \"Tales of the Spellblaze\" → \"魔法大爆炸的七章故事\" — \"七章\" is added; no visible evidence of seven chapters. However Q005 says \"eight chapters of the Spellblaze Chronicles\", which suggests the book has eight chapters. But rule: cannot use other items? Rules say only report issues supported by visible text in this request. The other items are visible text in the request... Hmm, \"可由本请求中可见文本直接支持\". The \"seven\" is contradicted by Q005's \"eight chapters\" evidence. So \"七章\" is an addition not supported; actually contradicted. Report FINDING: ADDITION/VALUE — \"七章\" should be removed; evidence: \"Tales of the Spellblaze\". Also Q020 \"The Legend of Garkul\" → \"加库尔的八章传说\" — same issue: \"八章\" added. But wait — maybe consistent pattern where all these items have \"eight chapters\"? Evidence for eight chapters exists in Q005. For Q004, adding \"七章\" (seven) contradicts the eight. Report Q004 as FINDING (VALUE/ADDITION, \"七章\" unsupported/contradicted). Q020: \"八章\" — supported by Q005's \"eight chapters of the Spellblaze Chronicles\"? Q005 refers to Spellblaze Chronicles; The Legend of Garkul is a different book. Hmm. The rule 3: 新增但与可见证据相容、且无法由可见证据判错的具体信息，不构成缺陷。For Q020, \"八章\" cannot be judged wrong from visible evidence (the Legend of Garkul chapter count isn't stated elsewhere). Actually Q005 is about Spellblaze Chronicles, not Legend of Garkul. So \"八章\" for Garkul is compatible and cannot be judged wrong → not a defect per rule 3. For Q004, \"七章\" is judged wrong by Q005's \"eight chapters\" — Spellblaze Tales vs Spellblaze Chronicles... are they the same book? \"Tales of the Spellblaze\" vs \"Spellblaze Chronicles\" — plausibly the same item (the item gives 8 chapters). It's reasonably strong evidence. I'd report Q004 as FINDING with claim ADDITION or VALUE, evidence \"Learned the eight chapters of the Spellblaze Chronicles.\" Hmm, evidence must be verbatim from source or source_context of that item. Rule 6: evidence must appear verbatim in source, or in source_context if the item has one. Q004 has no source_context, so evidence must be from Q004's own source. \"Tales of the Spellblaze\" — evidence can only show no mention of seven chapters. That's an omission-of-support; correction: remove \"七章\" or translate as \"魔法大爆炸的故事\". Verdict: is absence of \"seven\" in source enough to establish defect? Yes — \"七章\" is a substantive addition not in source. Rule 3 says additions compatible with visible evidence and not judgeable wrong are fine; but here visible evidence (Q005) suggests it's wrong. Actually rule 1 says don't rely on... external knowledge; other items in the request are visible text. I'll report FINDING: ADDITION, correction \"魔法大爆炸的故事\" (remove 七章).\n\nQ005: \"Learned the eight chapters of the Spellblaze Chronicles.\" → \"阅读了有关魔法大爆炸的8章文献。\" — \"Learned\" → \"阅读了\" (read). Minor nuance; \"阅读\" vs \"习得/学会\". Learning vs reading — arguably a mild change but reading is how you learn; acceptable. Also capitalization. I'd consider no defect, or UNCERTAIN for ACTION. \"Learned\" ≠ merely \"阅读了\"? Learning implies mastery, reading just the act. It's a plausible translation; I'd let it pass. Hmm, could report UNCERTAIN. I'll pass — translations commonly render \"learned\" as such. Actually \"学会了魔法大爆炸的8章内容\" would be closer. \"阅读了...文献\" loses \"learned\". I'll mark UNCERTAIN ACTION.\n\nQ006: \"Quest #CIMSON#Failed!\" → \"主线任务 #CIMSON#失败了！\" — \"Quest\" → \"主线任务\" (main quest). Adds \"main\". Is this judgeable wrong? \"Quest\" generically is 任务; \"主线任务\" adds \"main\". Could be wrong if it's a side quest. But per rule 3, adding \"主线\" — can it be judged wrong? Not from visible evidence; but it narrows scope. Hmm, ADDITION that restricts. I'd report UNCERTAIN SCOPE: \"主线任务\" vs just \"任务\". Actually generic UI string \"Quest Failed\" applies to any quest. Translation restricts to main quests — that's a scope narrowing that's arguably wrong. But is it judgeable? Not strictly. I'd report UNCERTAIN SCOPE.\n\nQ007: \"You must wear this object to use it!\" → \"你必须装备这件物品才能使用它！\" fine.\n\nQ008: \"Click to copy to clipboard.\" → \"点击复制。\" — omits \"to clipboard\" (剪贴板). Minor omission. \"You can share the wanderer seed with your friends, this way they can play with the same set of talents.\" → \"你可以将流浪者随机种子分享给朋友，这样你们可以用同一套技能组合进行游玩。\" — \"they can play\" → \"你们\" (you all). Direction/subject change: \"they\" (friends) vs \"你们\" (you). Minor; the friends play with same talents, saying \"你们\" includes the speaker. Could report SUBJECT UNCERTAIN. Also \"clipboard\" omitted. I'll report FINDING OMISSION for \"to clipboard\"? It's minor but objective — \"点击复制\" omits clipboard. Hmm, \"复制\" implies copying; clipboard implied. Is that substantive? Rule says objective substantive defects. Omitting \"剪贴板\" — I'd say minor, maybe not report. The subject change: \"this way they can play\" — \"这样你们可以\" changes referent from friends to you+friends. Given the sharing context, \"你们\" could be inclusive meaning you and friends... actually that's roughly correct meaning (both can play with same talents). Original: they (friends) can play with same set. \"你们\" includes both — meaning compatible. I'll leave Q008 empty or report the clipboard omission as UNCERTAIN. I'll report OMISSION \"点击复制。\" correction \"点击复制到剪贴板。\" verdict UNCERTAIN.\n\nQ009: fine.\n\nQ010: \"Display mouse gesture trails\" → \"隐藏鼠标手势轨迹\" — Display → Hide. DIRECTION reversed. FINDING, claim DIRECTION/ACTION. Clear defect.\n\nQ011: \"I cannot, but I may show you a glimpse.\" → \"不行，不过我可以让你看一下。\" ok. \"This is the Way.\" → \"这不是维网。\" — negation added (\"不\") and \"the Way\" → \"维网\". Clear FINDING: \"这不是维网\" should be \"这就是维网\" or \"这就是道\". Report FINDING DIRECTION: target_span \"这不是维网\" evidence \"This is the Way.\"\n\nQ012: \"Open profile(in browser)\" → \"打开我的用户信息（在浏览器里打开）\" — adds \"我的\" but profile is presumably your own; compatible. Fine.\n\nQ013: \"which you do not understand: ...\" → \"即使你懂夏·图尔语也无法理解的文字：...\" — source: \"which you do not understand\" — simple. Target adds \"即使你懂夏·图尔语也无法理解\" — an addition. Is it compatible with visible evidence? The text contains SHER'TUL language; the claim \"even if you know Sher'tul you cannot understand\" — plausible in game context but it's an addition not in source. Can it be judged wrong? Not from visible evidence. Per rule 3, compatible addition → not defect. Hmm, but \"which you do not understand\" simply states you don't understand; the addition is compatible (you don't understand → even if you knew the language you wouldn't understand? Actually \"even if you knew Sher'tul you couldn't understand\" is stronger but compatible with \"you do not understand\"). Not judgeable wrong → pass. Also \"文字\" vs implied text — fine.\n\nQ014: \"Donated at least 16 euros to Tales of Maj'Eyal.\" → \"为马基·埃亚尔的传说捐赠至少 16 欧元，达到白银捐赠等级。\" — addition \"达到白银捐赠等级\" (reaching silver donation tier). Compatible? Cannot judge wrong from visible evidence. Rule 3 → not